Today we launch Recursive. We are building AI that discovers knowledge automatically and improves itself recursively, an open-ended process that will fundamentally change how science and technology advance.

Our team of 25 top researchers and engineers in San Francisco and London brings diverse expertise in agentic AI scientists, architecture and algorithm design, world models, optimization, and interpretability, united by a shared conviction that this is the most important problem we could be working on today. If you are interested in joining, please send your resume to talent@recursive.com. Follow us at @Recursive_SI!

@Recursive_SI: "We are emerging from stealth with a bold bet on self-improving AI. We are former research team leaders from OpenAI, Google DeepMind, Meta AI, Salesforce AI, and Uber AI. We raised $650M at a $4.65 billion valuation to create AI that conducts experiments on how to..."

For the past few years, we've been considering the impact of open-endedness on the pursuit of AGI. Through our exploration, we've met talented researchers and engineers automating scientific discovery. When our top people decided to start a company together, we knew we had to be involved.

Coding agents may help implement experiments, but building automated research agents is going to be harder than most anticipate. This team can figure it out, and I always back Josh. You're stuck with me.

@josh_tobin_: "Today we’re launching Recursive (@Recursive_SI). We’re building AI that automates science, starting with the science of how to improve itself. One thing that has always stood out is how much progress depends on the experimental loop: deciding what to try, implementing it, running it, understanding what happened, and repeating. Recursive is automating that loop, safely and at scale. Excited to work on this with an incredible team across SF and London."

We can now interact with Gemini in real-time inside Genie worlds. Congratulations to @ashrewards and team on this exciting new direction.

@binks_stewart: "Project Genie is magical, but we've also been working on some new ways to interact with another player or agent. It was super fun to demo this at Google I/O, where attendees explored worlds with Gemini as a companion. We are excited to see how this can enable Gemini to learn how to interact with humans in embodied environments."

Examples of interacting with Gemini in these generated worlds are available in the video link.
